desc: ReaKit response-curve widget
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// rk_curve.jsfx-inc — a response curve in a plain rectangle: plate, log
// frequency grid, dB grid, an analyser underlay, the trace, and draggable
// nodes on top of it.
//
// Written clean-room for ReaKit. This has been written at least eight times
// inside plugins in this codebase and none of those was reusable: 4BandEQ's
// draw_curve is welded to eight filter objects and an image-slot cache,
// Compressor's and Gate's are inline in @gfx on linear dB axes, Saturation's
// is square and third-party licensed, Swing's per-pad EQ reads a dozen pad
// globals, and the Drum Strip's carries its own column cache. Externally the
// same story: ReEQ, RCModEQ and the Saike plugins each own a good curve that
// cannot leave its plugin. This one takes a rect and an array.
//
// ⭐ IT EVALUATES NOTHING. The caller fills an array with dB values and this
// draws it. No filter model, no FFT, no band abstraction — which is what lets
// the same widget serve an EQ response, a filter sweep, a compressor knee and
// a waveshaper transfer curve. It is also where the cost lives: filling that
// array per pixel per frame is how the Drum Strip once spent ~600k
// transcendentals a second while nothing moved. Fill it when something
// changes, not when something is drawn.
//
// ⭐ ONE X MAPPING, TWO Y MAPPINGS. The trace, the grid, the nodes and the
// analyser all call rk_curve_f2x, so they cannot drift apart — ReEQ keeps two
// parallel copies of its mapping and has a real (if harmless) typo where it
// assigns its right margin to its left. Y is deliberately NOT shared: a
// response is symmetric about the midline, an analyser runs absolute from a
// ceiling down to a floor.
//
// A face embedded in BOTH the TCP and the MCP runs @gfx twice a cycle, and the
// pass that does not own the mouse is handed the owner's pointer against its
// own, different, geometry. rk_curve_nodes does nothing on such a pass
// (`_srf_foreign`, the flag knobs_kbsg already honours; it reads 0 in a plugin
// that never sets it).
//
// REQUIREMENTS (host plugin must provide, per frame, before calling):
//   - mx, my       — mouse position
//   - lmb_down     — left-button PRESS edge (not the held state)
//   - LMB          — left-button HELD state
//   - CTRL, SHIFT  — modifier state (fine drag)
//   - dt, hv_speed — frame delta + hover ramp speed (as the other kbsg libs)
//
// MEMORY: the drawing calls claim NONE. rk_curve_nodes needs one caller-owned
// block of (8 + n) words for n nodes: [0] dragging index or -1, [1..2] the
// latched mouse origin, [3..4] the latched value origin, [5] the modifier
// state the latch was taken under, [6] last click time, [7] last click index,
// [8..8+n-1] per-node hover ramps.
//
// PUBLIC API:
//   rk_curve_init(fmin, fmax)
//        once, in @init. Precomputes the log denominator — 4BandEQ recomputes
//        log(20) and log(22000) inside its mapping on every pixel of every
//        frame. Defaults to 20..22000 if never called.
//   rk_curve_f2x(f, x, w) / rk_curve_x2f(px, x, w)      Hz <-> pixel
//   rk_curve_d2y(db, y, h, dr) / rk_curve_y2d(py, y, h, dr)
//        response dB <-> pixel, symmetric about the midline, full scale ±dr
//   rk_curve_a2y(db, y, h, ceil_db, floor_db)           analyser dB -> pixel
//   rk_curve_mag2db(m)         linear magnitude -> dB, floored at -140
//   rk_curve_plate(x, y, w, h, dr)
//        plate, frequency grid + labels, dB grid + labels, border. Sets a
//        small font for the labels and does not restore it.
//   rk_curve_plate_ex(x, y, w, h, dr, mode)
//        the same plate with the grid in `mode` (see rk_curve_grid).
//   rk_curve_font(px)
//        label size for the grid and the node readout. 0, the default, sizes
//        them from the rect's height, CAPPED at 11 px (x gfx_ext_retina). A
//        plugin that scales its whole face should pass its own scaled size.
//   rk_curve_grid(x, y, w, h, dr, lr, lg, lb, tr, tg, tb, mode)
//        the grid WITHOUT the plate, lines in one ink and labels in another,
//        for a graph that sits on a panel the plugin already painted. `mode` 0
//        is the full grid rk_curve_plate draws; 1 is compact -- the 0 dB line
//        and 100 / 1k / 10k, no labels -- for an embedded strip; 2 is the full
//        grid with EVERY dB line labelled but the floor's, for an EQ whose user
//        reads gain off the ruler (mode 0 labels only the top line and 0).
//   rk_curve_analyser(x, y, w, h, buf, n, f_lo, f_hi, ceil_db, floor_db, r, g, b)
//        the underlay. Call BEFORE the trace. f_lo/f_hi are the band buffer's
//        OWN frequency span — give it honestly and the bands land on the right
//        part of the graph whatever range the graph itself is set to.
//   rk_curve_trace(x, y, w, h, buf, n, dr, fill, r, g, b)
//        the curve. `fill` 1 shades to the midline.
//   rk_curve_trace_a(x, y, w, h, buf, n, dr, fill, r, g, b, a)
//        the same at alpha `a`, for a curve that dims while it is bypassed.
//   rk_curve_style(bands, nodes)
//        THE LOOK, one switch for everything below. bands: 0 Fill (each band a
//        shaded shape, no outline), 1 Lines (each band an outline, no shade),
//        2 both. nodes: 0 Ring (the band's name inside it), 1 Dot (a plain
//        disc), 2 Badge (a bigger disc, the name under it). Never called, it is
//        Fill and Ring. A plugin exposes the two as it likes; the default is the
//        same everywhere, which is what makes two EQs side by side agree.
//   rk_curve_draw(x, y, w, h, bbuf, n, nb, dr, cbuf, ebuf, hbuf, sel, dim,
//                 sbuf, tr, tg, tb, ta)
//        ⭐ THE ONE CALL for a multi-band response: every band's shade, the
//        0 dB line, the summed curve (`sbuf`, n points, colour tr,tg,tb at
//        alpha ta), and LAST every band's coloured outline, over the summed
//        curve. The ORDER lives here so no plugin can get it differently. The
//        band arguments are rk_curve_bands's, below.
//   rk_curve_bands(x, y, w, h, bbuf, n, nb, dr, cbuf, ebuf, hbuf, sel, dim)
//        the bands alone, all passes at once, for a graph with no summed curve.
//        EVERY band, in the current style, at the house alphas -- they live
//        here and nowhere else. `bbuf` is nb arrays of n dB values one after
//        another; `cbuf` r,g,b per band; `ebuf` 1/0 per band for on/off (pass
//        -1: all on); `hbuf` a 0..1 hover ramp per band (pass -1: none -- the
//        node block's mem + 8 is exactly this); `sel` the selected band or -1;
//        `dim` 1 while the whole EQ is bypassed.
//   rk_curve_bands_ex(..., what)
//        the same, one PASS at a time: what = 1 shades, 4 the 0 dB line,
//        2 outlines (add them). For a plugin that layers a second set of bands
//        and has to interleave the passes itself; everyone else: rk_curve_draw.
//   rk_curve_band(x, y, w, h, buf, n, dr, r, g, b, fill_a, line_a)
//        ONE band's own contribution, in that band's colour: a shade of it
//        filling to the midline at `fill_a`, and its outline at `line_a` (0
//        skips either). Call it once per band, before the summed trace. The
//        caller fills one array per band, the same way it fills the sum.
//   rk_curve_nodes(mem, x, y, w, h, dr, fbuf, dbuf, n)
//        the whole gesture: hover, grab, delta drag, wheel, double-click.
//        Publishes rk_curve_ni / rk_curve_nf / rk_curve_ndb / rk_curve_moved /
//        rk_curve_dblclk / rk_curve_hov / rk_curve_nwheel (wheel ticks over the
//        hovered node, consumed here so one tick is not eaten twice — that is
//        the library convention). WRITES NOTHING BACK — the caller
//        clamps and calls slider_automate, which is the one thing that made
//        every surveyed implementation unusable as a widget.
//   rk_curve_nodes_draw(mem, x, y, w, h, dr, fbuf, dbuf, n, sel, r, g, b)
//   rk_curve_nodes_draw_rgb(mem, x, y, w, h, dr, fbuf, dbuf, n, sel, cbuf)
//        the same nodes, each in its own colour. `cbuf` holds r, g, b per node,
//        three words each, in node order. Pairs with rk_curve_band.
//   rk_curve_nodes_draw_ex(mem, x, y, w, h, dr, fbuf, ybuf, n, sel, cbuf, ebuf, lblbuf)
//        the full painter. `ybuf` is where each node SITS in dB -- hand it the
//        real response at the node's frequency and a shelf's node rides the
//        curve instead of floating at twice its height. `ebuf` dims a band that
//        is off (-1: all on). `lblbuf` holds a string per node, its name
//        ("1", "LS"), or -1 for none.
//   rk_curve_node_paint_ex(nx, ny, rad, ht, on, al, r, g, b, lbl)
//        ONE node at a pixel, for a plugin that runs its own gesture and only
//        wants the look. ht 0..1 hover, on = selected or held, al = 1, or less
//        for a band that is off, lbl a string or -1. Sets a font.
//   rk_curve_node_label(nx, ny, rad, x, y, w, h, str)
//        a line of text by a node ("+3 dB Q1.2"): above it, below when that
//        would leave the rect, kept inside left and right. Sets a font.
//   rk_curve_palette(cbuf, n)
//        fills `cbuf` with the library's band colours for `n` bands (up to 6),
//        low to high, from red orange green teal blue violet. ONE definition,
//        so every plugin's low band is the same orange. Call once, in @init.
//   rk_curve_band_rgb(cbuf, i, c)
//        one slot, by hand: colour `c` (0 red .. 5 violet) into node `i`, for a
//        layout the palette does not guess -- a filter node, say.
//
// The TRACE buffer holds `n` dB values spread evenly across the rect in X, and
// because X is log frequency that is log spacing in Hz. ~512 is a good `n`.
//
// The ANALYSER buffer is different: it carries its own frequency span, because
// a meter's bands are whatever the meter chose and need not match the graph.
// Tell it that span and it places them correctly; assume they match and they
// silently stop matching the day either side changes.
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

@init

// ── Mapping ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

function rk_curve_init(fmin, fmax) (
  RKCV_FMIN = max(fmin, 1);
  RKCV_FMAX = max(fmax, RKCV_FMIN * 2);
  rkcv_lmin = log(RKCV_FMIN);
  rkcv_flog = log(RKCV_FMAX) - rkcv_lmin;     // precomputed once, not per pixel
);

rk_curve_init(20, 22000);

function rk_curve_f2x(f, x, w) (
  x + w * (log(max(f, RKCV_FMIN)) - rkcv_lmin) / rkcv_flog;
);

function rk_curve_x2f(px, x, w) (
  exp(rkcv_lmin + (px - x) / max(w, 1) * rkcv_flog);
);

// Response: symmetric about the midline, ±dr full scale.
function rk_curve_d2y(db, y, h, dr) (
  y + h * 0.5 - db / max(dr, 0.0001) * h * 0.5;
);

function rk_curve_y2d(py, y, h, dr) (
  (y + h * 0.5 - py) / max(h * 0.5, 1) * dr;
);

// Analyser: absolute, ceiling at the top, floor at the bottom.
function rk_curve_a2y(db, y, h, ceil_db, floor_db) local(t) (
  t = (db - ceil_db) / min(floor_db - ceil_db, -0.0001);
  y + h * min(max(t, 0), 1);
);

function rk_curve_mag2db(m) (
  m < 0.0000001 ? -140 : 8.685889638065035 * log(m);
);

// ── Plate and grid ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// The nine-stop ladder every EQ in the corpus converges on.
function rk_curve_grid_f(i) (
  i == 0 ? 20   : i == 1 ? 50   : i == 2 ? 100  : i == 3 ? 200 :
  i == 4 ? 500  : i == 5 ? 1000 : i == 6 ? 2000 : i == 7 ? 5000 : 10000;
);

function rk_curve_font(px) ( rkcv_font = max(0, px); );
// The default was 7.5% of the height with no ceiling: on a 290 px graph that is
// 21 px, bigger than the plugin's own title, and the ruler shouted over the
// curve it was there to measure. Capped at 11 px, doubled with the surface on a
// HiDPI display (gfx_ext_retina reads 0 in a plugin that never sets it).
function rk_curve_font_px(h) (
  rkcv_font > 0 ? rkcv_font
                : min(max(8, floor(h * 0.075)), floor(11 * max(1, gfx_ext_retina)));
);

// The grid alone, in the caller's inks. rk_curve_plate is a plate, this, and a
// border -- so a graph on its own plate and a graph on a themed panel still put
// every line and every label in the same place.
function rk_curve_grid(x, y, w, h, dr, lr, lg, lb, tr, tg, tb, mode)
local(i, f, gx_, gy_, tw, th, last_x, db) (
  mode == 1 ? (
    // Compact: the midline, and a decade ladder. An embedded strip has no room
    // for labels and nine verticals read as hatching at that size.
    gfx_set(lr, lg, lb, 0.70);
    gfx_rect(x, rk_curve_d2y(0, y, h, dr), w, 1);
    gfx_set(lr, lg, lb, 0.40);
    f = 100;
    loop(3,
      f > RKCV_FMIN && f < RKCV_FMAX ? gfx_rect(rk_curve_f2x(f, x, w), y, 1, h);
      f *= 10;
    );
  ) : (
    gfx_setfont(1, "Arial", rk_curve_font_px(h));

    // Frequency grid. Labels sit INSIDE the rect: outside they get clipped by
    // whatever the caller has drawn around the graph.
    last_x = -1000;
    i = 0;
    loop(9,
      f = rk_curve_grid_f(i);
      f > RKCV_FMIN && f < RKCV_FMAX ? (
        gx_ = rk_curve_f2x(f, x, w);
        gfx_set(lr, lg, lb, 0.40);
        gfx_rect(gx_, y, 1, h);
        f >= 1000 ? sprintf(#rkcv_s, "%dk", f / 1000) : sprintf(#rkcv_s, "%d", f);
        gfx_measurestr(#rkcv_s, tw, th);
        // Suppress a label that would touch the previous one.
        gx_ - tw * 0.5 > last_x + 4 ? (
          gfx_set(tr, tg, tb, 0.95);
          // Keep it inside the plate: a stop close to either end would otherwise
          // hang half its label off the edge.
          gfx_x = min(max(gx_ - tw * 0.5, x + 2), x + w - tw - 2);
          gfx_y = y + h - th - 2;
          gfx_drawstr(#rkcv_s);
          last_x = gfx_x + tw;
        );
      );
      i += 1;
    );

    // dB grid: five lines at i*(dr/2), 0 dB brighter than the rest.
    i = -2;
    loop(5,
      db = i * dr * 0.5;
      gy_ = rk_curve_d2y(db, y, h, dr);
      gfx_set(lr, lg, lb, i == 0 ? 0.70 : 0.35);
      gfx_rect(x, gy_, w, 1);
      // Top and midline only. The bottom line shares the rect's floor with the
      // frequency labels, and -dr printed there lands straight on top of them --
      // the scale is symmetric, so +dr and 0 already say what the bottom is.
      // Mode 2 adds the two quarter lines: a working EQ is read off its ruler,
      // and the 4-Band and Apex both labelled every line before they drew
      // through this. A half step (15 dB range -> 7.5) keeps its decimal; the
      // 4-Band used to print that one as "+8".
      i == 2 || i == 0 || (mode == 2 && i != -2) ? (
        // 0 is "0": "%+d" prints "+0", which is nobody's idea of zero.
        db == 0 ? strcpy(#rkcv_s, "0") :
        mode == 2 && db != floor(db) ? sprintf(#rkcv_s, "%+.1f", db) : sprintf(#rkcv_s, "%+d", db);
        gfx_measurestr(#rkcv_s, tw, th);
        gfx_set(tr, tg, tb, 0.85);
        gfx_x = x + 3;
        gfx_y = i == 2 ? gy_ + 2 : gy_ - th - 1;   // top line labels below itself
        gfx_drawstr(#rkcv_s);
      );
      i += 1;
    );
  );
);

function rk_curve_plate_ex(x, y, w, h, dr, mode) (
  gfx_set(0.043, 0.047, 0.067, 1);
  gfx_rect(x, y, w, h);

  rk_curve_grid(x, y, w, h, dr, 0.18, 0.20, 0.25, 0.42, 0.45, 0.52, mode);

  gfx_set(0.20, 0.22, 0.27, 1);
  gfx_rect(x, y, w, 1); gfx_rect(x, y + h - 1, w, 1);
  gfx_rect(x, y, 1, h); gfx_rect(x + w - 1, y, 1, h);
);

function rk_curve_plate(x, y, w, h, dr) ( rk_curve_plate_ex(x, y, w, h, dr, 0); );

// ── Analyser underlay ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Filled to the bottom, quads guarded on an integer column change so many
// bands landing in one pixel do not each emit a degenerate triangle.

function rk_curve_analyser(x, y, w, h, buf, n, f_lo, f_hi, ceil_db, floor_db, r, g, b)
local(i, x0, x1, y0, y1, bot, t, j, fr, fx, lspan) (
  n < 2 ? 0 : (
    bot = y + h - 1;
    lspan = log(max(f_hi, f_lo * 2) / max(f_lo, 1));
    // ⭐ Bands are placed BY FREQUENCY, not by spreading them evenly across the
    // rect. The first version assumed the buffer spanned the whole graph, which
    // was true only while the analyser and the graph happened to share a range
    // — and the moment the meter's span changed with srate, every band moved.
    // Each pixel asks what frequency it is and fetches the band for it, so the
    // two align however either one is configured.
    gfx_set(r, g, b, 0.13);
    i = 0;
    loop(floor(w),
      fx = rk_curve_x2f(x + i, x, w);
      fx >= f_lo && fx <= f_hi ? (
        t  = log(fx / max(f_lo, 1)) / lspan * (n - 1);
        j  = floor(t);
        fr = t - j;
        j < 0 ? ( j = 0; fr = 0; );
        j >= n - 1 ? ( j = n - 2; fr = 1; );
        y0 = rk_curve_a2y(buf[j] + (buf[j + 1] - buf[j]) * fr, y, h, ceil_db, floor_db);
        y0 < bot ? gfx_rect(x + i, y0, 1, bot - y0);
      );
      i += 1;
    );
    // Capping line, same placement, so the edge and the fill agree.
    gfx_set(r, g, b, 0.32);
    i = 1;
    loop(floor(w) - 1,
      fx = rk_curve_x2f(x + i, x, w);
      fx >= f_lo && fx <= f_hi ? (
        t  = log(fx / max(f_lo, 1)) / lspan * (n - 1);
        j  = floor(t); fr = t - j;
        j < 0 ? ( j = 0; fr = 0; );
        j >= n - 1 ? ( j = n - 2; fr = 1; );
        y0 = rk_curve_a2y(buf[j] + (buf[j + 1] - buf[j]) * fr, y, h, ceil_db, floor_db);
        y1 = rk_curve_a2y(buf[j], y, h, ceil_db, floor_db);
        gfx_rect(x + i, y0, 1, 1);
      );
      i += 1;
    );
  );
);

// ── The trace ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

function rk_curve_trace_a(x, y, w, h, buf, n, dr, fill, r, g, b, a)
local(i, x0, x1, y0, y1, cy_, top, bot, t, j, fr) (
  n < 2 ? 0 : (
    top = y + 1; bot = y + h - 2; cy_ = y + h * 0.5;

    // ⭐ ONE VERTICAL LINE PER PIXEL. Quads were the obvious way to do this and
    // they were wrong twice: with a pixel-column guard they skipped segments and
    // the fill came out striped, and without it, adjacent quads share an edge
    // that the polygon fill does not paint, so a hairline gap appeared between
    // every pair -- the same stripes, from the opposite cause. A column per
    // pixel cannot gap and cannot overlap, and it costs one loop over the width.
    fill ? (
      gfx_set(r, g, b, 0.14 * a);
      i = 0;
      loop(floor(w),
        t  = i / max(w - 1, 1) * (n - 1);
        j  = floor(t);
        fr = t - j;
        j >= n - 1 ? ( j = n - 2; fr = 1; );
        y0 = rk_curve_d2y(buf[j] + (buf[j + 1] - buf[j]) * fr, y, h, dr);
        y0 = min(max(y0, top), bot);
        gfx_rect(x + i, min(cy_, y0), 1, max(1, abs(y0 - cy_)));
        i += 1;
      );
    );

    // Shadow pass then the line itself: one pixel down and dark, so the trace
    // reads against the grid without needing a thicker stroke.
    gfx_set(0, 0, 0, 0.45 * a);
    i = 0;
    loop(n - 1,
      x0 = x + i * w / (n - 1);
      x1 = x + (i + 1) * w / (n - 1);
      y0 = min(max(rk_curve_d2y(buf[i],     y, h, dr), top), bot);
      y1 = min(max(rk_curve_d2y(buf[i + 1], y, h, dr), top), bot);
      gfx_line(x0, y0 + 1, x1, y1 + 1);
      i += 1;
    );
    gfx_set(r, g, b, a);
    i = 0;
    loop(n - 1,
      x0 = x + i * w / (n - 1);
      x1 = x + (i + 1) * w / (n - 1);
      y0 = min(max(rk_curve_d2y(buf[i],     y, h, dr), top), bot);
      y1 = min(max(rk_curve_d2y(buf[i + 1], y, h, dr), top), bot);
      gfx_line(x0, y0, x1, y1);
      i += 1;
    );
  );
);

function rk_curve_trace(x, y, w, h, buf, n, dr, fill, r, g, b) (
  rk_curve_trace_a(x, y, w, h, buf, n, dr, fill, r, g, b, 1);
);

// ── One band, in its own colour ───────────────────────────────────────
// A multi-band curve reads as BANDS when each keeps a colour: its node, and a
// shade of the same colour filling that band's own bell under the summed
// trace. The FILL skips every column shorter than half a pixel, so a narrow
// band costs only the pixels it covers. The OUTLINE is drawn wherever the band
// is off the midline and SNAPS onto the midline's own pixel row where it lands;
// along the midline itself rk_curve_bands draws ONE neutral line, full width,
// for all of them. See the note in the outline pass for why it is not simply
// every band's colour run edge to edge.
function rk_curve_band(x, y, w, h, buf, n, dr, r, g, b, fill_a, line_a)
local(i, j, t, fr, x0, x1, y0, y1, cy_, top, bot, ay, a0, a1) (
  n < 2 ? 0 : (
    top = y + 1; bot = y + h - 2; cy_ = y + h * 0.5;
    fill_a > 0 ? (
      gfx_set(r, g, b, fill_a);
      i = 0;
      loop(floor(w),
        t  = i / max(w - 1, 1) * (n - 1);
        j  = floor(t);
        fr = t - j;
        j >= n - 1 ? ( j = n - 2; fr = 1; );
        y0 = rk_curve_d2y(buf[j] + (buf[j + 1] - buf[j]) * fr, y, h, dr);
        y0 = min(max(y0, top), bot);
        abs(y0 - cy_) >= 0.5 ? gfx_rect(x + i, min(cy_, y0), 1, abs(y0 - cy_));
        i += 1;
      );
    );
    // The outline. FOUR tries, in order, so the next person does not repeat one:
    //  1. each line simply stopped half a pixel from the midline. Beside a 0 dB
    //     gridline too faint to see, it looked like it ended in mid-air.
    //  2. a 4 px fade into the midline. A bell's tail nears 0 dB so slowly that
    //     4 px of height is a long stretch of graph: every line went murky.
    //  3. every band's colour run edge to edge, "like Apex". Looked at closely
    //     the midline was a dashed candy stripe two rows thick: each tail sits a
    //     fraction of a pixel above or below 0 dB, so neighbours land on
    //     different rows, and being translucent they mix differently all the way
    //     along. (Apex never showed it: it floored every coordinate and drew
    //     opaque, so its last band simply painted the whole line.)
    //  4. THIS: a band's colour wherever it is off the midline, its end SNAPPED
    //     onto the midline's exact pixel row (`ay`, the row rk_curve_grid's 0 dB
    //     line occupies), and the stretch along the midline left to the one
    //     neutral line rk_curve_bands draws full width. Nothing hangs, nothing
    //     stripes, and the 0 dB line does not claim to be any one band's.
    line_a > 0 ? (
      gfx_set(r, g, b, line_a);
      ay = floor(cy_);
      // "On the midline" is within 1.5 px, not 0.5. A Gaussian demo bell dies
      // away fast; a REAL filter's skirt stays a few hundredths of a dB off 0
      // for octaves, which is a coloured line one pixel from the 0 dB line for
      // half the graph, antialiased INTO its row: the neutral line came out
      // tinted a different colour every few hundred Hz (seen on the 4-Band and
      // Apex, 2026-09-21). 1.5 px keeps an outline clear of that row or on it.
      i = 0;
      loop(n - 1,
        y0 = min(max(rk_curve_d2y(buf[i],     y, h, dr), top), bot);
        y1 = min(max(rk_curve_d2y(buf[i + 1], y, h, dr), top), bot);
        a0 = abs(y0 - cy_) < 1.5;
        a1 = abs(y1 - cy_) < 1.5;
        !(a0 && a1) ? (
          a0 ? y0 = ay;
          a1 ? y1 = ay;
          x0 = x + i * w / (n - 1);
          x1 = x + (i + 1) * w / (n - 1);
          gfx_line(x0, y0, x1, y1);
        );
        i += 1;
      );
    );
  );
);

// ── The look ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Three ways to show a band and three ways to show a node were each alive in
// one EQ and nowhere else: shaded shapes in EON Tone, thick outlines in Apex,
// a named ring in the 4-Band, a plain dot in Tone, a badge in Apex. They are
// all here now, so a plugin chooses a look instead of owning one -- and every
// plugin starts on the SAME one. Zero is the default on purpose: a plugin that
// never calls this, and memory that starts zeroed, both land on Fill and Ring.
RKCV_FILL = 0; RKCV_LINES = 1; RKCV_BOTH = 2;
RKCV_RING = 0; RKCV_DOT   = 1; RKCV_BADGE = 2;
RKCV_BADGE_K = 1.12;      // a Badge disc's radius, as a multiple of the node radius

function rk_curve_style(bands, nodes) (
  rkcv_sty_bands = min(max(floor(bands), 0), 2);
  rkcv_sty_nodes = min(max(floor(nodes), 0), 2);
);

// Every band, in the current style. ⭐ THE ALPHAS LIVE HERE. Before this each
// EQ typed its own (0.20, 0.50, 0.13 ...), which is why the same filter looked
// like three different filters in three plugins.
//   fill  0.20, rising to 0.34 under the pointer or selected
//   line  0.40 beside a fill, 0.85 alone; hover and selection lift it
//   off   a band that is switched off keeps a ghost of its shape, so it can
//         still be found: fill 0.06, line 0.18
//   axis  in the two styles that draw outlines, ONE neutral line along 0 dB,
//         edge to edge, that every outline runs into (0.55)
//   dim   everything x 0.35 while the whole EQ is bypassed
//
// ⭐ ORDER. Shades, then the 0 dB line, then the SUMMED curve, then the coloured
// outlines ON TOP of it. A band that overlaps no other IS the sum over its own
// span, so with the outlines underneath the white curve hid every one of them
// and "Fill + Lines" looked exactly like "Fill" (user, 2026-09-21: "draw the
// colored outlines over the white curve"). Hence passes: rk_curve_draw runs
// them in that order with the trace in the middle.
function rk_curve_bands_ex(x, y, w, h, bbuf, n, nb, dr, cbuf, ebuf, hbuf, sel, dim, what)
local(i, en, ht, fa, la, k, r, g, b) (
  k = dim ? 0.35 : 1;
  i = 0;
  loop(nb,
    en = ebuf < 0 ? 1 : ebuf[i];
    ht = hbuf < 0 ? 0 : hbuf[i];
    i == sel ? ht = max(ht, 0.7);
    fa = rkcv_sty_bands == RKCV_LINES ? 0 : en ? 0.20 + 0.14 * ht : 0.06;
    la = rkcv_sty_bands == RKCV_FILL  ? 0 :
         rkcv_sty_bands == RKCV_LINES ? ( en ? 0.85 + 0.15 * ht : 0.18 )
                                      : ( en ? 0.40 + 0.40 * ht : 0.18 );
    r = cbuf[i * 3]; g = cbuf[i * 3 + 1]; b = cbuf[i * 3 + 2];
    (what & 1) && fa > 0 ? rk_curve_band(x, y, w, h, bbuf + i * n, n, dr, r, g, b, fa * k, 0);
    (what & 2) && la > 0 ? (
      rk_curve_band(x, y, w, h, bbuf + i * n, n, dr, r, g, b, 0, la * k);
      // Alone, a one-pixel outline is too thin to carry a colour: a second pass
      // a pixel lower gives it weight without a polygon.
      rkcv_sty_bands == RKCV_LINES && en ?
        rk_curve_band(x, y + 1, w, h, bbuf + i * n, n, dr, r, g, b, 0, la * k * 0.45);
    );
    i += 1;
  );
  // The line the outlines run into: on the same pixel row the outlines snap to
  // and the grid's own 0 dB line sits on. Under the summed curve, which is
  // white where it lies flat along it.
  (what & 4) && rkcv_sty_bands != RKCV_FILL ? (
    gfx_set(0.62, 0.66, 0.74, 0.55 * k);
    gfx_rect(x + 1, floor(y + h * 0.5), w - 2, 1);
  );
);

function rk_curve_bands(x, y, w, h, bbuf, n, nb, dr, cbuf, ebuf, hbuf, sel, dim) (
  rk_curve_bands_ex(x, y, w, h, bbuf, n, nb, dr, cbuf, ebuf, hbuf, sel, dim, 7);
);

function rk_curve_draw(x, y, w, h, bbuf, n, nb, dr, cbuf, ebuf, hbuf, sel, dim,
                       sbuf, tr, tg, tb, ta) (
  rk_curve_bands_ex(x, y, w, h, bbuf, n, nb, dr, cbuf, ebuf, hbuf, sel, dim, 5);
  rk_curve_trace_a(x, y, w, h, sbuf, n, dr, 0, tr, tg, tb, ta);
  rk_curve_bands_ex(x, y, w, h, bbuf, n, nb, dr, cbuf, ebuf, hbuf, sel, dim, 2);
);

// The band colours, in one place: the six the EON EQs wear (Swing's master and
// per-pad EQ, EON Tone). They run in FREQUENCY order, low to high like a
// spectrum, so neighbouring bands never share a hue -- two bands whose fills
// overlap in the same colour read as one shape.
//   0 red   1 orange   2 green   3 teal   4 blue   5 violet
// The ends belong to the filters (HPF red, LPF violet) and teal to a second
// mid, so fewer than six bands take the MIDDLE of the run, the way those EQs do:
//   3 -> orange green blue          4 -> orange green teal blue
//   5 -> red orange green blue violet          6 -> all of them
function rk_curve_band_rgb(cbuf, i, c) (
  c == 0 ? ( cbuf[i * 3] = 0.92; cbuf[i * 3 + 1] = 0.30; cbuf[i * 3 + 2] = 0.30; ) :
  c == 1 ? ( cbuf[i * 3] = 0.96; cbuf[i * 3 + 1] = 0.60; cbuf[i * 3 + 2] = 0.18; ) :
  c == 2 ? ( cbuf[i * 3] = 0.38; cbuf[i * 3 + 1] = 0.86; cbuf[i * 3 + 2] = 0.36; ) :
  c == 3 ? ( cbuf[i * 3] = 0.22; cbuf[i * 3 + 1] = 0.82; cbuf[i * 3 + 2] = 0.70; ) :
  c == 4 ? ( cbuf[i * 3] = 0.30; cbuf[i * 3 + 1] = 0.66; cbuf[i * 3 + 2] = 0.96; ) :
           ( cbuf[i * 3] = 0.68; cbuf[i * 3 + 1] = 0.44; cbuf[i * 3 + 2] = 0.96; );
);

function rk_curve_palette(cbuf, n) (
  n <= 1 ? ( rk_curve_band_rgb(cbuf, 0, 2); ) :
  n == 2 ? ( rk_curve_band_rgb(cbuf, 0, 1); rk_curve_band_rgb(cbuf, 1, 4); ) :
  n == 3 ? ( rk_curve_band_rgb(cbuf, 0, 1); rk_curve_band_rgb(cbuf, 1, 2);
             rk_curve_band_rgb(cbuf, 2, 4); ) :
  n == 4 ? ( rk_curve_band_rgb(cbuf, 0, 1); rk_curve_band_rgb(cbuf, 1, 2);
             rk_curve_band_rgb(cbuf, 2, 3); rk_curve_band_rgb(cbuf, 3, 4); ) :
  n == 5 ? ( rk_curve_band_rgb(cbuf, 0, 0); rk_curve_band_rgb(cbuf, 1, 1);
             rk_curve_band_rgb(cbuf, 2, 2); rk_curve_band_rgb(cbuf, 3, 4);
             rk_curve_band_rgb(cbuf, 4, 5); ) :
           ( rk_curve_band_rgb(cbuf, 0, 0); rk_curve_band_rgb(cbuf, 1, 1);
             rk_curve_band_rgb(cbuf, 2, 2); rk_curve_band_rgb(cbuf, 3, 3);
             rk_curve_band_rgb(cbuf, 4, 4); rk_curve_band_rgb(cbuf, 5, 5); );
);

// ── Nodes ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

function rk_curve_node_r(w) ( max(5, min(w * 0.020, 11)); );

// Nearest node within reach, or -1. Scanning all of them and taking the
// nearest is the difference between this and 4BandEQ's first-come-wins, which
// grabs the wrong node the moment two overlap.
function rk_curve_nodes_hit(x, y, w, h, dr, fbuf, dbuf, n)
local(i, nx, ny, d, best, bi, rad) (
  rad = rk_curve_node_r(w) * 2.4;
  best = rad * rad; bi = -1;
  i = 0;
  loop(n,
    nx = rk_curve_f2x(fbuf[i], x, w);
    ny = rk_curve_d2y(dbuf[i], y, h, dr);
    d = (mx - nx) * (mx - nx) + (my - ny) * (my - ny);
    d < best ? ( best = d; bi = i; );
    i += 1;
  );
  bi;
);

function rk_curve_nodes(mem, x, y, w, h, dr, fbuf, dbuf, n)
local(i, hov, mods, dx, dy, sens, now) (
  rk_curve_ni = -1; rk_curve_moved = 0; rk_curve_dblclk = 0;
  rk_curve_nf = 0;  rk_curve_ndb = 0;  rk_curve_nwheel = 0;

  // A foreign pass (see the header) holds the owner's pointer against THIS
  // pass's rect: it must not hover, grab, drag or eat the wheel. It must not
  // drop the drag either -- LMB there is the owner's held button.
  // ⚠️ A held node publishes its CURRENT frequency and gain on such a pass. A
  // caller is entitled to gate its write-back on "a node is held" (mem[0] >= 0)
  // rather than on rk_curve_moved -- the 4-Band does, deliberately -- and with
  // these left at zero that caller would write 20 Hz / 0 dB into the dragged
  // band on every foreign frame. The current value written back is a no-op.
  _srf_foreign ? (
    rk_curve_hov = mem[0];
    mem[0] >= 0 ? (
      rk_curve_ni  = mem[0];
      rk_curve_nf  = fbuf[mem[0]];
      rk_curve_ndb = dbuf[mem[0]];
    );
  ) : (

  hov = mem[0] >= 0 ? mem[0] : rk_curve_nodes_hit(x, y, w, h, dr, fbuf, dbuf, n);
  rk_curve_hov = hov;

  i = 0;
  loop(n,
    i == hov ? ( mem[8 + i] = min(mem[8 + i] + dt * hv_speed, 1); )
             : ( mem[8 + i] = max(mem[8 + i] - dt * hv_speed, 0); );
    i += 1;
  );

  // Wheel over a node. Whoever consumes frame_wheel zeroes it, so a single
  // tick is not also read by whatever is drawn next.
  hov >= 0 && frame_wheel != 0 ? (
    rk_curve_nwheel = frame_wheel;
    rk_curve_ni = hov;
    frame_wheel = 0;
  );

  mods = (CTRL ? 1 : 0) + (SHIFT ? 2 : 0);

  // Grab. Latch the mouse AND the value it started from, so the drag is a
  // delta: grabbing an off-centre node must not teleport it under the cursor.
  hov >= 0 && lmb_down && mem[0] < 0 ? (
    now = time_precise();
    now - mem[6] < 0.25 && mem[7] == hov ? (
      rk_curve_dblclk = 1; rk_curve_ni = hov;
      mem[6] = 0;                      // consume it, so it is not seen twice
    ) : (
      mem[6] = now; mem[7] = hov;
      mem[0] = hov; mem[1] = mx; mem[2] = my;
      mem[3] = fbuf[hov]; mem[4] = dbuf[hov]; mem[5] = mods;
    );
  );

  !LMB ? mem[0] = -1;

  mem[0] >= 0 ? (
    i = mem[0];
    // Re-latch whenever the modifiers change, or the node jumps by the
    // difference in sensitivity at the instant the key goes down.
    mods != mem[5] ? (
      mem[1] = mx; mem[2] = my;
      mem[3] = fbuf[i]; mem[4] = dbuf[i]; mem[5] = mods;
    );
    // CTRL fine, SHIFT extra-fine -- the same ladder knobs_kbsg and
    // sliders_kbsg document. This read 0.35 for SHIFT, which made it
    // COARSER than CTRL and contradicted both headers.
    sens = CTRL ? 0.10 : SHIFT ? 0.025 : 1;
    dx = (mx - mem[1]) * sens;
    dy = (my - mem[2]) * sens;
    rk_curve_ni  = i;
    rk_curve_nf  = rk_curve_x2f(rk_curve_f2x(mem[3], x, w) + dx, x, w);
    rk_curve_ndb = rk_curve_y2d(rk_curve_d2y(mem[4], y, h, dr) + dy, y, h, dr);
    rk_curve_moved = (dx != 0 || dy != 0);
  );

  );   // !_srf_foreign
  rk_curve_ni;
);

// One node, in the current style. Every painter below calls it, so a node
// looks the same whichever one drew it and they cannot drift apart.
//   Ring   the 4-Band's: a disc in the band's colour, a dark centre, the band's
//          name inside it. With no name, the centre is smaller and carries a dot.
//   Dot    EON Tone's: a plain disc with a dark outline.
//   Badge  a bigger solid disc with white rims, the name INSIDE it in dark
//          type. (It came from Apex with the name underneath, because Apex
//          fills the disc with a filter-shape picture. With nothing inside, the
//          disc was an empty blob over the peak it marked and the name was a
//          speck below it. Apex passes no name and draws its picture and its
//          number itself, sized from RKCV_BADGE_K.)
// `al` scales every alpha, for a band that is switched off.
function rk_curve_node_paint_ex(nx, ny, rad, ht, on, al, r, g, b, lbl)
local(tw, th, br) (
  rkcv_sty_nodes == RKCV_DOT ? (
    ht > 0.01 ? ( gfx_set(r, g, b, 0.22 * ht * al); gfx_circle(nx, ny, rad * 1.7, 1, 1); );
    gfx_set(r, g, b, al);
    gfx_circle(nx, ny, rad * 0.85, 1, 1);
    gfx_set(0, 0, 0, 0.40 * al);
    gfx_circle(nx, ny, rad * 0.85, 0, 1);
    on ? ( gfx_set(1, 1, 1, 0.90 * al); gfx_circle(nx, ny, rad * 0.85 + 2, 0, 1); );
  ) : rkcv_sty_nodes == RKCV_BADGE ? (
    br = rad * RKCV_BADGE_K;
    ht > 0.01 ? ( gfx_set(r, g, b, 0.18 * ht * al); gfx_circle(nx, ny, br * 1.6, 1, 1); );
    gfx_set(r, g, b, al);
    gfx_circle(nx, ny, br, 1, 1);
    gfx_set(1, 1, 1, 0.55 * al); gfx_circle(nx, ny, br, 0, 1);
    gfx_set(1, 1, 1, 0.22 * al); gfx_circle(nx, ny, br - 1, 0, 1);
    gfx_set(0, 0, 0, 0.85 * al); gfx_circle(nx, ny, br + 1, 0, 1);
    on ? ( gfx_set(1, 1, 1, 0.80 * al); gfx_circle(nx, ny, br + 3, 0, 1); );
    lbl >= 0 ? (
      // the name, dark on the band's colour
      gfx_setfont(1, "Arial", max(7, floor(rad * 1.05)), 'b');
      gfx_measurestr(lbl, tw, th);
      // Dark on the band's colour -- unless the band is off, when the disc is
      // dimmed almost to the plate and dark type on it vanishes ("HP" / "LP" on
      // the 4-Band's switched-off filters). Then it is light.
      al < 0.6 ? gfx_set(0.92, 0.94, 0.98, 0.70) : gfx_set(0.04, 0.05, 0.07, 0.92 * al);
      gfx_x = nx - tw * 0.5 + 0.5; gfx_y = ny - th * 0.5;
      gfx_drawstr(lbl);
    );
  ) : (
    // hover halo
    ht > 0.01 ? (
      gfx_set(r, g, b, 0.16 * ht * al);
      gfx_circle(nx, ny, rad * 1.9, 1, 1);
    );
    // body, dark core, ring
    gfx_set(r, g, b, (on ? 1 : 0.78) * al);
    gfx_circle(nx, ny, rad, 1, 1);
    gfx_set(0.04, 0.04, 0.06, 0.85 * al);
    gfx_circle(nx, ny, rad * (lbl >= 0 ? 0.74 : 0.55), 1, 1);
    gfx_set(1, 1, 1, (on ? 0.95 : 0.55) * al);
    gfx_circle(nx, ny, rad, 0, 1);
    lbl >= 0 ? (
      // the name, where the centre dot would be
      gfx_setfont(1, "Arial", max(7, floor(rad * 0.95)), 'b');
      gfx_measurestr(lbl, tw, th);
      gfx_set(1, 1, 1, (on ? 1 : 0.88) * al);
      gfx_x = nx - tw * 0.5 + 0.5; gfx_y = ny - th * 0.5;
      gfx_drawstr(lbl);
    ) : on ? (
      gfx_set(1, 1, 1, 0.9 * al);
      gfx_circle(nx, ny, max(1, rad * 0.18), 1, 1);
    );
  );
);

function rk_curve_node_paint(nx, ny, rad, ht, on, r, g, b) (
  rk_curve_node_paint_ex(nx, ny, rad, ht, on, 1, r, g, b, -1);
);

// A line of text by a node. Above it; below when above would leave the rect.
function rk_curve_node_label(nx, ny, rad, x, y, w, h, str)
local(tw, th, ty) (
  gfx_setfont(1, "Arial", rk_curve_font_px(h));
  gfx_measurestr(str, tw, th);
  ty = ny - rad * 1.4 - th - 2;
  ty < y + 1 ? ty = ny + rad * 1.4 + 2;
  gfx_set(0.86, 0.88, 0.92, 0.95);
  gfx_x = min(max(nx - tw * 0.5, x + 2), x + w - tw - 2);
  gfx_y = ty;
  gfx_drawstr(str);
);

function rk_curve_nodes_draw(mem, x, y, w, h, dr, fbuf, dbuf, n, sel, r, g, b)
local(i, rad, held) (
  rad = rk_curve_node_r(w);
  held = mem[0];
  i = 0;
  loop(n,
    rk_curve_node_paint(rk_curve_f2x(fbuf[i], x, w), rk_curve_d2y(dbuf[i], y, h, dr),
                        rad, mem[8 + i], i == sel || i == held, r, g, b);
    i += 1;
  );
);

function rk_curve_nodes_draw_rgb(mem, x, y, w, h, dr, fbuf, dbuf, n, sel, cbuf)
local(i, rad, held) (
  rad = rk_curve_node_r(w);
  held = mem[0];
  i = 0;
  loop(n,
    rk_curve_node_paint(rk_curve_f2x(fbuf[i], x, w), rk_curve_d2y(dbuf[i], y, h, dr),
                        rad, mem[8 + i], i == sel || i == held,
                        cbuf[i * 3], cbuf[i * 3 + 1], cbuf[i * 3 + 2]);
    i += 1;
  );
);

function rk_curve_nodes_draw_ex(mem, x, y, w, h, dr, fbuf, ybuf, n, sel, cbuf, ebuf, lblbuf)
local(i, rad, held, top, bot) (
  rad = rk_curve_node_r(w);
  held = mem[0];
  // A node riding a curve that is clamped to the rect stays inside it too.
  top = y + 1; bot = y + h - 2;
  i = 0;
  loop(n,
    rk_curve_node_paint_ex(rk_curve_f2x(fbuf[i], x, w),
                           min(max(rk_curve_d2y(ybuf[i], y, h, dr), top), bot),
                           rad, mem[8 + i], i == sel || i == held,
                           ebuf < 0 || ebuf[i] ? 1 : 0.30,
                           cbuf[i * 3], cbuf[i * 3 + 1], cbuf[i * 3 + 2],
                           lblbuf < 0 ? -1 : lblbuf[i]);
    i += 1;
  );
);
